 d_Y(y,y')=\alpha\lambda D(\pi(y),\pi(y'))
 \qquad(y,y'\in Y).$$ In particular, $$\label{comp:endpoint-modulus}
 \lim_{r\downarrow0}\limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \sup_{\substack{u,v\in V_n\\\rho(z_n(u),z_n(v))\le r}}
 \delta_n(u,v)=0.$$

If $\alpha=0$, there are unique points $y_0\in Y$ and $z_0\in\widehat{\mathbb C}$ such that $$\label{comp:cross-relation}
 R=(\{y_0\}\times\widehat{\mathbb C})\ \cup\ (Y\times\{z_0\}).$$ Moreover $z_0$ is a measurable function of the limiting distance graph $\mathcal K(R)$ alone. It is the unique point $z$ for which $$\label{comp:positive-diagonal}
 (z,z,r)\in\mathcal K(R)\quad\hbox{for some }r>0.$$
